  "query_preview": "..Morning satellite imagery showed debris clouds \nfrom last nights convection is slowly decreasing across the \nforecast area. At the surface, dewpoints ranged from 40s and \nlower 50s east of an Eloy to Nogales line while west of the line\nvalues were mostly in the 20s. PWAT values, per satellite,\nranged 0.45\" to 0.85\".\n\nToday will be a little less active versus yesterday with the\nchance of afternoon/evening showers/storms being E and S of \nTucson. Main threat from storms will be gusty outflow winds, \nblowing dust and brief moderate to heavy rain. Can't rule out a \nsevere wind gust, especially across southern Graham/Greenlee \ncounties. Once again made adjustments to PoPs from the baseline\nNBM. \n\nMoisture pushes out of the area on Friday, but enough around for\na slight chance of storms across the White mountains. \n\nThe weekend into next week will be dry with high temperatures\naround normal the area between trofiness to the W/NW and\nhigh pressure to the E/SE.\n\n8-14 day outlooks (June 11-17)\nTemperature: Likely above normal (54%-56% chance) \nPrecipitation: Near normal (36% chance)",
